/**
\page org_mitk_views_fiberquantification Fiber Quantification

This view provides tools to derive additional information (such as tract density images and principal fiber direction maps) from tractograms.

\section SecFiberQuantInput Input Data

- Tractogram: The input streamlines.
- Reference Image: The output images will have the same geometry as this reference image (optional). Only its grid is used, so any 3D image, also a diffusion-weighted image, can serve as reference.

Without a reference image, all fiber-derived images and principal fiber directions use the same grid, which covers the bounding box of the tractogram. For the fiber-derived images, the upsampling factor divides each voxel of the reference image or of this grid into factor x factor x factor voxels; the principal fiber directions are not upsampled.

\section SecFDI Fiber-derived Images

- Tract density image: The voxel values correspond to the sum of all fiber segment lengths in the respective voxel, each weighted by the weight of its fiber.
- Normalized TDI: 0-1 normalized version of the TDI.
- Binary envelope: Generate a binary image (1 in every voxel a fiber passes) from the input tractogram.
- Fiber bundle image: Generate an RGBA image of the fiber directions (colour) and the fiber length (opacity) in each voxel, both weighted by the fiber length in the voxel and the fiber weights.
- Fiber endings image: Generate a 3D image that counts the fiber endpoints in each voxel.
- Fiber endings pointset: Generate a pointset containing the locations of fiber endpoints (not recommended for large tractograms).
- Distance map: Signed distance (mm) to the border of the binary envelope, positive inside the envelope.
- Binary skeleton: The binary envelope thinned to its skeleton.
- Streamline visitation count: Number of streamlines that pass through each voxel; each streamline counts once per voxel (track density imaging of Calamante et al. 2010).
- Fiber pointset: Generate a pointset containing all points of all fibers (not recommended for large tractograms).

\section SecPD Principal Fiber Directions

Calculate the voxel-wise principal fiber directions (fixels) from a tractogram, on the grid of the reference image if one is selected.
- Max. Peaks: Maximum number of output directions per voxel (at least 1).
- Angular Threshold: Cluster directions that are close together using the specified threshold (in degree).
- Size Threshold: Discard principal directions whose size is smaller than this fraction of the largest direction in the voxel. The size of a direction is the summed length of the fiber segments clustered into it, weighted by the fiber weights. The clustering can count a segment for two neighbouring clusters; when these are merged into one direction, the segment counts twice in its size.
- Normalization: Normalize the principal fiber directions by the global maximum, the voxel-wise maximum or each direction individually.
- Output \#Directions per Voxel: Generate an image that contains the number of principal directions per voxel as values.

\section SecJudging Judging a Tractogram

Numbers that tell whether a tractogram is a plausible tract: the fiber count (a dense tract of a single bundle has hundreds to thousands of fibers; a handful means that the ROIs or the cutoff cut it), the fiber lengths (minimum, mean, median and maximum along the streamlines: the arcuate fasciculus is 100 to 130 mm long, the corticospinal tract from the brain stem to the cortex about 120 mm, the uncinate 40 to 60 mm; a mean far below the expected length means fibers that stop early), the volume of the voxels the fibers pass (the binary envelope: a tract of several millilitres, not a thread of a few voxels), and the fibers that pass, end in or end with both ends in given ROIs (fibers ending outside the expected regions suggest a missing not ROI). The view's binary envelope and fiber endings image show the same in images.

The REST endpoint POST /api/v1/diffusion/tractography/metrics returns these numbers for a tractogram and optional ROIs (binary images or labels of a segmentation) without changing the data storage: {fibers, length_mm: {min, mean, median, max}, volume_ml, rois: [{name, fibers_passing, fibers_ending, fibers_both_ends}]}.

\imageMacro{DirectionExtractionFib.png, "Input fiber bundle",10}
\imageMacro{DirectionExtractionPeaks.png, "Output principal fiber directions",10}

*/
